      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14155694/observations/lesson-step-589680-c897ebf0d86c2c8a1ecb.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14155694/observations/lesson-step-589680-c897ebf0d86c2c8a1ecb.png",
      "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/topics/6691",
      "captured_at": 1791860874.0868106,
      "math_academy_id": "e-21720",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving an Inhomogeneous System Using a Given Matrix Exponential",
      "topic_id": "6691",
      "source_placement_id": 589680
    },
    {
      "dom_id": "step-589683",
      "name": "Example: \n                    Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System",
      "problem": "Consider the inhomogeneous system  $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ passing through $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$ is presented below.\n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)$  \n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}'(t) = -Ae^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t) + e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}'(t)$  \n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}'(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(t)$  \n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{y}(t)-\\mathbf{y}(t_0) = \\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$ \n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$  \n\n$\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n\n$\\textrm{L8}{:}\\;$ $e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L9}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n\nFill in the blanks with the correct reasons to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from $\\textrm{L2}$ by  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14155694/assets/a57b16a4b4392ad68547e3192dfde0c795d11e960c5100e14cd54c275df18415.svg).\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14155694/assets/281de511438021a8e37a938beb7039bb060a44f944da15128634c8a037f9830e.svg).\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L8}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14155694/assets/62aae06fe282decc2963be0ca8d0018b30fac355624b46226e93d27033a6e2e2.svg).",
      "worked_solution": "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\textrm{L2}$ and $\\textrm{L3}.$ Starting from\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}'(t) = -Ae^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t) + e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}'(t)\n$$\n\nin $\\textrm{L2}$ and substituting $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)$ from the original system gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}(Ax(t)+g(t)) \\\\  & =-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from $\\textrm{L2}$ by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14155694/assets/83729359deb05033193c0efafb53f3d3164d7974543a2549eb63276bbd834311.svg)\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L1}$ and $\\textrm{L5}.$ Evaluating $\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)$ at $t=t_0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=e^{-A(t_0-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=I\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$  by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14155694/assets/7e6ad910e9037e26dc2fd533bbab660b6c6f90e48b05975b071390d6063a9219.svg)\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L1}$ and $\\textrm{L8}.$ Multiplying both sides of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nfrom $\\textrm{L1}$ by $e^{A(t-t_0)}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t) & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t) \\\\  & =x(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L8}$ follows from $\\textrm{L1}$ by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14155694/assets/759ebf5f590b98c90b82384ce915060a412eeb20af8f9fe0949dacb9a1b9980f.svg)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
